course: dictionary
lesson: 2947
title: One word, many meanings: drift
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The words around it show you which meaning the writer wants. Look at the clues before you decide.
explain: «The boat began to drift away from the shore.» Nobody is steering the boat, and it is on water, so drift means to be carried slowly along without control.
explain: «The boys drift off, muttering.» Here drift means to go away slowly, without a plan. Test it: swap in "slowly wander away" and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Drift can also be a noun. «The snow made a deep drift against the wall» means a big pile of snow that the wind has blown. When you meet a word you know, check whether the sentence wants a thing or an action.
fact: Drift can be a verb (to drift, drifts, drifted, drifting) or a noun (a drift, drifts).
fact: To drift means to be carried slowly by water or air, without control.
fact: A drift is a big pile of snow or sand blown by the wind.
fact: «Continents drift a few centimetres a year, about as fast as fingernails grow.»
fact: The drift of what someone says is its general meaning, so «get the drift» means you understand the main idea.
exercise: «The leaf began to drift across the pond.» What does drift mean here? | Sink to the bottom | Shine brightly | Break into tiny pieces | *Be carried slowly along by water or air
exercise: «The road was blocked by a huge snowdrift.» What is a snowdrift? | Rain falling in the night | A fast car sliding on ice | A bird's nest | *A big pile of snow blown by the wind
exercise: Ella says, «I didn't quite get the drift of the story.» What does drift mean here? | A pile of sand | A slow, careless walk | *The general meaning
test: «The raft began to drift away from the shore.» What does drift mean? | Sink straight down | *Be carried slowly away by the water | Sail fast with a plan | Stay perfectly still
test: «After the long day, Sam began to drift off to sleep.» What does drift off mean? | *Slowly fall asleep | Eat a big supper | Wake up with a shout | Run quickly to bed
exam: Which sentence uses drift as a noun? | «Continents drift apart.» | *«The snow made a drift against the wall.» | «The owl drifts over the crowd.» | «The boat began to drift.»
